   107	focuses on scrutinizing and characterizing the security of GitHub
   108	Actions in comparison to other CI/CD platforms [4, 24].
   109	An important aspect neglected by the existing literature are the
   110	computing resources consumed on the platform. In particular, it
   111	is currently unknown how many computing resources projects
   112	typically consume, what these resources are used for, and what
   113	kinds of optimization opportunities exist. Obtaining insights into
   114	resource usage and optimization opportunities in workflows on
   115	GitHub Actions helps understand their impact on development
   116	costs, and ultimately, contributes toward cheaper and faster CI/CD.
   117	This paper presents the first empirical study that analyzes computational resource usage and optimization opportunities of workflows. Our study is based on a dataset of 952 repositories that
   118	performed 1.3 million workflow runs over a period of 30 months,
   119	which is the largest such dataset we are aware of. The study is
   120	driven by three research questions:
   121	**RQ1: What amount of resources do workflows use and what**
   122	**are these resources used for?** The average annual cost of running
   123	workflows for a paid-tier repository in our dataset is approximately
   124	$504, with the majority of executed jobs running on the least expensive Linux machines. Pull requests, pushes, and scheduled events
   125	
