   128	
   129	
   130	
   131	are the most common triggering events for workflows, accounting
   132	for more than 90.0% of the total VM time. The most frequently
   133	performed tasks are testing and building. While most workflows
   134	complete successfully, 10.0% to 17.4% fail, primarily due to errors
   135	in code during testing or building. These results show the considerable costs incurred by CI/CD platforms, which motivates studying
   136	possible optimizations.
   137	**RQ2: To what extent do workflows use currently available**
   138	**optimization mechanisms, and what is their impact on the**
   139	**consumed resources?** We study and analyze both the prevalence
   140	and impact of six mechanisms that could help reduce the cost of
   141	running workflows. The mechanisms range from sophisticated
   142	optimizations, such as caching, which is used by 32.9% of paid-tier
   143	repositories and reduces VM time by 3.4%, to simple configuration
   144	options, such as setting a timeout value for a job, which is used by
   145	14.0% of paid-tier repositories, impacts 4.3% of runs, and reduces
   146	VM time by 8.1%.
