   151	These findings show that there still are significant opportunities
   152	for reducing both computational and monetary costs.
   153	The results of this study could interest at least two groups of
   154	people. On the one hand, it will help developers in reducing the time
   155	taken by their CI/CD pipelines, which decreases waiting time and
   156	speeds up the development process. In addition, repositories that
   157	require resources beyond those provided for free by GitHub have
   158	an interest in reducing the monetary costs imposed by workflows.
   159	On the other hand, our results can guide platform providers, such
   160	as GitHub, when reducing their costs, especially the costs imposed
   161	by free-tier repositories.
   162	In summary, this paper presents the following contributions:
   163	
   164	
   165	(1) The first study of the resource usage of workflows on GitHub
   166	Actions, which differs from prior work that studies static
   167	properties of workflows [7, 9, 23, 39].
   168	(2) A quantitative analysis of existing and potential optimizations of CI/CD pipelines that shows the (potential) prevalence and impact of ten different optimizations.
   169	(3) Actionable insights on how to reduce the computational
   170	resources consumed by workflows, which is relevant for
   171	both developers and platform providers.
   172	(4) A reusable dataset of 1.3 million workflow runs from 952
   173	repositories, which can serve as a basis for further analysis
   174	and novel optimization techniques.
